n it's about  representation it's about people people  feeling safe and secure and dignified um  over the past 40 days relatively  security situation have not been making  headlines and i have been hearing a lot  of people globally international leaders  that i meet um they tell me that it's  relatively secure or stable but for me  that's not stability and plus in  addition to that you have just heard  about the horrible attack which targets  the civilians it's endless and it's  heartbreaking jonathan it's  heartbreaking for us who have basically  we are born  in war and we will some of us die in war  how long how long should the people of  afghanistan actually pay this price i  think it's the crime  for all of us as international community  and as a global community rather  to act together um uh in the face of  what's happening in afghanistan and  listen to the people of afghanistan in  this in this case yeah and you've been  very critical of course  of the manner of the withdrawal um by  western troops you were shot by the  taliban  um but you still sat down with them to  try  to negotiate um what are your fears now  for women and girls in the country  well the reason i actually wanted to  negotiate with them was uh to make them  understand that afghanistan has  transformed that woman and the younger  generation of afghanistan are part of  that transformation and unavoidable uh  partners you know generation of the  society that cannot be avoided they need  to the taliban in this case need to  listen to the people of ramadan and  accept the fact that they can live only  with you know tolerance and coexistence  we could form a political settlement the  taliban would have been one  uh element of that but of course there  were other political and social groups  and religious minorities and women that  could live together however  was managed and the fact that the  taliban basically expanded by their  military strategy and favored that over  a political settlement we a